WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.513
SQL injection prevention rests on a
precise boundary.

2
00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:06.410
Trusted application code defines the SQL
program,

3
00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.485
and externally controlled values travel
through the database client's

4
00:00:10.565 --> 00:00:12.334
parameter-binding mechanism.

5
00:00:12.414 --> 00:00:15.296
Concatenation, ordinary template
interpolation,

6
00:00:15.376 --> 00:00:19.699
and raw fragments break that boundary when
they insert untrusted text into the

7
00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:20.434
statement.

8
00:00:20.514 --> 00:00:25.099
TypeScript interfaces, browser validation,
and authentication do not repair it.

9
00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:29.502
Manual escaping is fragile and should not
replace the driver's parameter API.

10
00:00:29.582 --> 00:00:34.037
Use structured ORM and query-builder
operations according to their documented

11
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:38.767
semantics, and treat raw, unsafe, literal,
and fragment features as review points.

12
00:00:38.847 --> 00:00:43.236
Stored procedures require the same
discipline when they construct dynamic SQL

13
00:00:43.316 --> 00:00:44.036
internally.

14
00:00:44.116 --> 00:00:48.308
Ordinary values such as identifiers stored
in columns, dates, quantities,

15
00:00:48.388 --> 00:00:50.288
and statuses can usually be bound.

16
00:00:50.368 --> 00:00:54.233
SQL identifiers, operators, directions,
and clauses are structure;

17
00:00:54.313 --> 00:00:55.426
when they must vary,

18
00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:59.436
map a client choice to a small closed set
of server-authored fragments.

19
00:00:59.516 --> 00:01:04.036
Build complex searches by accumulating
trusted predicates and parameter values

20
00:01:04.116 --> 00:01:04.836
separately.

21
00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:09.436
Generate placeholder positions from code
and collections, never from raw input.

22
00:01:09.516 --> 00:01:12.333
Give empty arrays, optional filters,
pagination,

23
00:01:12.413 --> 00:01:14.771
and report structure explicit semantics.

24
00:01:14.851 --> 00:01:18.912
Keep runtime validation, authorization,
tenant scope, workflow rules,

25
00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:22.660
error handling, and least-privilege
database accounts in place.

26
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.736
They complement parameterization and do
not become substitutes for it.

27
00:01:26.816 --> 00:01:30.680
Finally, make raw query paths easy to
find, review, test, and monitor.

28
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.773
Verify the binding behavior of the actual
library API,

29
00:01:33.853 --> 00:01:38.111
test unexpected values and structural
choices, and assert protected state.

30
00:01:38.191 --> 00:01:42.580
A secure query remains safe when every
external value contains unexpected data

31
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.328
because none of those values is allowed to
rewrite the SQL program.

32
00:01:46.408 --> 00:01:50.600
That is the engineering invariant to
preserve across drivers, frameworks,

33
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.300
ORMs, query builders, and future
refactoring.
